<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Nagaoka Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1618 CE – 1871 CE · capital Nagaoka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Echigo domain whose leader Kawai Tsugunosuke fought one of the hardest battles of the Boshin War against the imperial army in 1868.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> gave Nagaoka in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/echigo-province">Echigo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makino-tadanari">Makino Tadanari</a> in 1618, with about 74,000 koku. His descendants ruled until the end of the period, and the domain's code of spartan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> behaviour was proverbial.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nagaoka controlled the middle course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinano-province">Shinano</a> river and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata">Niigata</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> took it over in 1843. In the 1860s the senior retainer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawai-tsugunosuke">Kawai Tsugunosuke</a> reformed finances and the army, buying modern rifles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gatling-gun">Gatling guns</a>, and sought armed neutrality between the shogunate and the new government.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the imperial army refused talks in 1868, Nagaoka joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ouetsu-reppan-domei">Ouetsu Reppan Domei</a>. Its castle fell, was retaken and fell again; Kawai died of wounds. In 1870 the retainer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobayashi-torasaburo">Kobayashi Torasaburo</a> used a gift of a hundred sacks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> to found a school, a story later famous. It became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata-prefecture">Niigata Prefecture</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nagaoka-domain.jpg" alt="Nagaoka Domain" width="320" height="249"><figcaption>Image: Barfodt · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Makino_Lords_of_Nagaoka_in_Echigo_Province_(B).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1618 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nagaoka</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Makino Tadanari, Kawai Tsugunosuke</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Japan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nagaoka han</dd></dl></aside></div>
